> 
> This is just *too* damned weird -- isn't it interesting the way the
> universe keeps throwing this sort of thing at you?  Guess I'll need
> to read the math and see if I can figure out what's happening here.
> Not to rain on any parades, but are there any obviously
> insurmountable engineering problems here?  E.g. the wormholes are
> grossly unstable;

Traversable wormholes, as outlined by Visser, Thorne and Morris et al
are not unstable.  As classical objects they are stable (providing
you can confine the negative energy as specified).  As quantum objects
they become unstable when someone attempts to transform them into time
machines.  There are wormholes that *are* classically unstable - for
that reason they are not traversable.  The wormhole pinches off as you
fly down it and ... well no-one's quite sure what happens next, but it
involves hitting a singularity and you certainly don't get out the other
side.

There is a good comparison of traversable and non-traversable wormholes
in American Journal of Physics v56, p395 (1988)  Morris and Thorne.

> they produce excessive tidal forces;

Not across the faces of the polyhedrons.  According to Visser the faces
remain stress and tidal free.

> they have sub-atomic dimensional tolerances

The edges don't have any special requirement like being infinity thin
cylinders or anything, so my guess is they don't require nano-scale
tolerances.  Another reason for guessing this is the polyhedron doesn't
have to be regular.  Any old shape will do, it's just the faces are
easier for us folks to slip through.

> ... and what exactly is negative energy?

Ah!  But what is energy?  I don't know the answer to either question. 
But negative energy exists (eg Inflation and Casimir energies are both
negative).  There's a good overview of how physicists' conceptions of
the various energy conjectures have evolved this century in the Am J
Phys ref above.  The Am J Phys article is a very chatty and informative
article in general (although it's getting a bit out of date on the AWEC
front).
> 
> This area, of course, is great news for hard SF writers.

SF has *lead* the scientists.  Carl Sagan started off all this
traversable wormhole speculation and research by sending proofs of
Contact (his novel) to the Am J Phys authors (and others), asking for
feedback.  Sagan incorporated the prelimimary results in his novel.  The
authors suggest students read Contact as a light introduction to
traversable wormholes!

