Date: Thu, 8 Apr 93 13:05:25 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: TECH: Truely "Boundless" Expansion

If all goes well, civilization should be able to expand many many
orders of magnitude from where we are now, colonizing the solar
system, galaxy, galaxy clusters, etc.  But eventually, after we have
expanded up to the scale of the universe we can observe, "boundless"
expansion may get much harder.

Now most of us may find it hard to get worked up worrying about
our prospects after this point, but some extropian physicists are
seriously concerned about whether physics limits our expansion to
some finite, though very large value, or whether we can have truely
infinite expansion.

I've been reading some physics papers on this, and discussing issues
with Mike Price (who I hope will correct me if I err), and now will
summarize for the rest of you.

First, let's make some distinctions.  All future life from here on out
can be broken into "descendants", each of which has for all practical
purposes lost contact with the others; each is in a seperate
"universe".  Each descendant has a size, an amount of stuff,
particularly negentropy, which it can use to represent its current
state, its memories, its achievements, etc.  And each can have a
lifetime, a subjective number of state-changes it can go through.  All
of the properties can be finite, bounded from above by some specific
limit, or infinite, growing without bound.  Finally, we might
distinguish a coherence time, the subjective time it takes for signals
to cross from one end of a descendant to another and back again.

When each descendant has finite stuff, then there are in some sense
only a finite number of distinct descendants.  Even with infinite
time, if a descendant has only a finite number of possible states it
can be in, it will eventually return exactly to a previous state, and
history will repeat itself from there.  With an infinite number of
such descendants, there might be a infinite number of exact copies of
each possible civilization, but this seems finite in the sense that
matters.

A plain vanilla universe is either open or closed.  If closed, it has
a finite size and will collapse in a finite time, giving a finite
number of finite-stuff descendants with finite subjective lives.  Most
have estimated the maximum entropy (log # states) of such a universe
to be similar to that of a black hole of similar size.  Frank Tipler,
however, speculates [1] that the boundary condition of quantum gravity
might directly require that life be infinite in the senses he prefers,
so that an infinite future awaits civilization even after the universe
has collapsed smaller than the plank radius.  Mike and I find this
unconvincing (though consistent); Tipler has simply assumed his
conclusion.

A plain vanilla open universe can give a single infinite stuff
descendant with infinite subjective life.  Freeman Dyson has suggested
[2] that life would be wise to lower its tempurature and rate of
subjective experience as the universe cooled.  If life could keep
porting itself to hardware running at lower and lower tempuratures,
then it could get an infinite subjective life, by creating an infinite
amount of entropy, with only a finite initial amount of free energy
per unit/stuff.  The amount of stuff available to use would grow as
the logarithm of subjective time; infinite, yes, but it might not feel
that way.  If there were some minimum tempurature that life would not
function below, then infinite life might still be possible here, if
the unbounded amount of stuff could be taken advantage of.  

Two kinds of exotic matter can change the above pictures.  First, we
might find or create materials with tension (negative pressure)
greater than its mass.  Such materials can induce inflation, where
the universe expands exponentially, without substantially diluting the
density of stuff at each point, until the inflation falls apart as the
exotic stuff turns into ordinary matter.

With natural chaotic inflation [3], there must be distant regimes
still undergoing inflation, and if we start soon enough and move fast
enough, and try in all directions at once, some of us might reach
these regimes before they stop inflating, and ride the inflation to
new vast universes to inhabit.  If some of us could keep repeating the
trick at each stage, our descendants could continue to exponentially
multiply indefinitely.  However, each descendant would be
finite-sized, limited to the size of their (almost certainly closed)
inflation generated-universe at best, and at worst to whatever can fit
in the largest colonizing vehicles.

If we could create our own "basement" universes, inducing inflation in
a "laboratory", there would be no problem finding new inflating
universes, but each one would still be finite, and we might expect
much stricter limitations on the "genome", the number of bits that
could be passed on to a descendant universe before it pinched off and
became inaccessible.

Another kind of exotic matter would have high tension and negative
mass, and may allow wormholes; though it may also not.  Wormholes are
like "portals", with two ends following different space-time
trajectories.  You jump in one end, at one time along the trajectory,
and you come out at the other end.  The event on the other trajectory
where you come out is the place where that end has aged just as much
as this end, relative to when the wormhole ends were created together.
If you make the ends age differently, by accelerating one to near the
speed of light, for example, and then bring them close together again,
you might make a time machine, though current indications are that all
wormholes collapse (or perhaps repel each other) as they approach
becoming a time machine.  Alternatively, if you just keep sending the
other end away, you can directly connect the here and now to
arbitrarily distant parts of space.

If we found naturally occuring wormholes, and there were enough of
them, then we might expand out into an open universe, inflating or
not, without needing to attend to that pesky speed of light limit.
Available stuff might expand exponentially as a function of subjective
experience.  Unfortunately it seems unlikely that many randomly
created wormholes could avoid forming net time machines, and then all
collapsing away.

If we could make our own wormholes, then we might carefully coordinate
them to avoid forming time machines, and send ends out at near the
speed of light.  This doesn't help in a closed universe, but in an
open one it might allow the amount of available stuff to expand
arbitrarily fast as a function of subjective lifetime, and for
arbitrarily small coherence times.  Combining wormholes and inflation,
we might colonize and connect an infinity of inflated regimes into a
single exponentially expanding descendant.

[1] Frank Tipler, "The ultimate fate of life in universes which undergo
    inflation", Phys Lett B 286 (1992) 36-43.

[2] Freeman Dyson, "Time without end: Physics and biology in an open
    universe", Rev Mod Phys, V51, N3, July 1979, p447-460.

[3] A.D. Linde "Life After Inflation" Phys Lett B, V211, N1,2 25Aug88,
    p29,30. 

