(1) Yes, I forgot about the putative clay-like system.  In this case,
the clay-system was completely supplanted by the DNA-system (and
perhaps there was an RNA-system in the middle).  And in the (near?)
future, the DNA-system may be supplanted by some sort of purely memic
evolution.  But for the time being, our memes live as "parasites" on
hardware which is itself evolving, which leads to:

(2) It seems "obvious" that genetic evolution is slow on the scale of
recorded history.  (Although one should watch out -- recorded history
spans only 6,000 years or so, agriculture goes back another 4,000
years, and reasonably modern humans might go back another 40,000 or
100,000 years.  That's over an order of magnitude more time.)  But
I've always wondered how true that is.  Historically, infant mortality
rates have been around 50%.  And the variance among men in the number
of children they father in polygamous societies is very high.  (It
might be high in monogamous societies, too, if we account for
paternity correctly!)  It seems to me that the potential natural
selection is very strong, and we're talking about something like 240
generations over recorded history.  A 10% per generation attrition of
a gene gives overall attrition of something like 10^-10.

(One could add the selective effects of wars and other deliberate
violence.  In Japan, for 1,000 years or so, a warrior who was offended
by a peasant or other lowly type could cut him down at will.  That
would tend to select out any genetic propensity toward insolence!  And
the death rates among men due to warfare in warrior cultures is high.)

And at least some personality traits seem to have a genetic component.
Some have been measured to have a strong genetic component.  Many seem
to be fixed by a very young age, which argues for some genetic
component.

*If* significant genetic evolution could have happened over the time
spans of cultural evolution, it leads to interesting speculations
about the interactions of genes and memes -- they replicate
independently, so they don't have the same interests.  Genes could
evolve to prevent themselves from hosting harmful memes; memes could
evolve to avoid these defenses.  *There might be thoughts you are
unable to think.*  (Or maybe you can think them only in the most
abstract, symbolic way, and not in an emotional, motivating way.)

I thought all of this was the merest speculation, of course, until I
ran into a thought you must think: The unity of consciousness.  We all
run around thinking that we have a "consciousness", and that it is a
"thing".  But all the evidence of science is that the human mind is an
information-processing system with many parts.  More interestingly, I
know that I have sensory evidence that my mental functions are not
unitary, but I still perceive my consciousness to be unitary:

One day, due to a sudden drop in my blood pressure, I managed to erase
my short-term memory.  After blood flow was restored, my short-term
memory re-booted from some sort of non-volatile medium-term memory.
(No big deal.)  But in the mean time, I had walked over a non-trivial
path, and one that was not a habit with me, but rather had been
decided upon just before the crash of my conscious mind.  What it
showed is that there is an autonomous "motion-planning" part of my
brain that takes care of walking around obstacles, turning corners,
etc., once the path has been given to it by the conscious mind.  In
fact, I must deal with the motion planner minute-by-minute every day
of my life.  But until its existence was revealed in this way, I was
never "aware" that it existed, despite using it habitually.

Even experimentally-induced partitions of consciousness are not
perceived by the subject, even though the subject's conscious behavior
is tuned to compensate for the parition:

Split-brain patients never report having any sort of consciousness
that there are two independent brains living in their skulls, despite
that the two brains develop very clear tricks to keep each other
informed of what is going on.  (Often, the two brains cook up tricks
that defeat split-brain experimenters who are trying to figure out
what differences there are between left-brains and right-brains!)

So I propose that the "unity of consciousness" is a hardwired idea of
the human brain, and that the possibility of our genes waging war on
our memes is not pure speculation...

*That* should launch a thousand flames!

Dale

