Let me reply by way of a parable --

Suppose you want to find out about the extreme forms of Christianity,
the ones that could best be described as Zealot Christianity ("I've
found the One True Way!").  Not that I have anything in particular
against Christian Zealots, but they're pretty common in our culture,
so there's a better chance that you all will see what I mean if I use
them as examples.

If you talk to a Zealot, they will immediately refer you to several
books, which they recommend with great zeal.  And many of them will
recommend the same books.  But those books are usually Zealot books,
written by Zealots for Zealots.  For some strange reason, non-Zealots
don't like them and aren't convinced by them.  And if you read a
Zealot book, you will discover that while you can't exactly find the
flaws in its reasoning, it always leaves you with a dissatisfied
feeling, like something has somehow been overlooked.

And the more Zealots who recommend a book, the worse it will be.
Zealots like a book because it confirms and supports their belief in
Zealotry.  To make a *lot* of Zealots like a book, it has to put the
best possible spin on Zealotry.

The one thing a Zealot book won't discuss is the actual history of
Zealotry, the applications of its ideas in the real world, and their
consequences.  Or rather, the discussion will be *very selective*.  As
Bob Black noted, "Christians have insisted that their behavior over
the last 1900 years or so shouldn't be held against Christianity."  So
they don't base the justification of Zealotry on the track record of
Zealotry.  They set up a (necessarily simplified) social analysis, and
show that Zealotry will produce the best of all possible results.
Which is all rather scary, since the social reformers of the 1960s
(and all previous batches of them as well) have discovered that it is
one thing to install changes in society, and quite another to have
those changes produce the results one wants...

The way to find a good book on Zealotry is to find one about which
anti-Zealot will say "I disagree with every conclusion in this book,
but it's a good analysis of Zealotry."  If one can't find that,
another couple of good signs are whether the book contains a detailed
critique of all known examples of Zealotry in the real world, and
whether it outlines the circumstances under which the presented system
of social organization *won't* work.  (For any social system, one can
usually find situation in history that is sufficiently perverted to
destroy it.)

-- end of parable

